<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deutschlandlied</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words <a href="/years/1841">1841</a>; melody <a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Das Lied der Deutschen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The melody is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-haydn">Joseph Haydn</a>'s, written in 1797 for Lorenz Leopold Haschka's &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</a>&quot;, a birthday anthem for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Emperor Francis II</a> modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot;. It remained the anthem of the Austrian emperors, with new words from 1854, until the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> in 1918.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The song ceased to be an anthem when the GDR was dissolved and its states joined the Federal Republic of Germany at reunification in 1990, and the &quot;<b>Deutschlandlied</b>&quot; became the anthem of united Germany.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auferstanden-aus-ruinen">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</a></li><li><blockquote>Like the &quot;<b>Deutschlandlied</b>&quot; and Ernst Moritz Arndt's &quot;Was ist des Deutschen Vaterland?&quot;, and unlike &quot;Heil dir im Siegerkranz&quot;, which praised a monarch, it called on Germans to set aside the rivalries of their many states and unite.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;Horst-Wessel-Lied&quot; was the anthem of the Nazi Party (NSDAP) from 1930 to 1945, and from 1933 to 1945 the Nazi regime made it Germany's second national anthem, sung after the first stanza of the &quot;<b>Deutschlandlied</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied</a></li><li><blockquote>The German-controlled Protectorate of Bohemia and Moravia (1939–1945) kept the song as its anthem, usually played together with the German anthems, the <b>Deutschlandlied</b> and the Nazi Horst-Wessel-Lied.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kde-domov-muj">Kde domov můj</a></li></ul></section>
